each\n\n$$\nX∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $X∧¬Y.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b19ad54e-7fbb-5207-bf8a-3ab93b802ddf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b19ad54e-7fbb-5207-bf8a-3ab93b802ddf" :answer-field/id #uuid "b19ad54e-7fbb-5207-bf8a-3ab93b802ddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3b04d6d-0c18-5c36-91f6-4f598bc216d8" "answer-5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" "answer-7bffc6ae-a7c2-591c-bc55-a77e46655d6f" "answer-b621cd70-8e5a-56ee-b272-20c6098bad66" "answer-9233ceaf-9f1c-5208-b9cd-674ecda2cce9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3b04d6d-0c18-5c36-91f6-4f598bc216d8" :answer/id #uuid "f3b04d6d-0c18-5c36-91f6-4f598bc216d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" :answer/id #uuid "5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bffc6ae-a7c2-591c-bc55-a77e46655d6f" :answer/id #uuid "7bffc6ae-a7c2-591c-bc55-a77e46655d6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "X∨¬Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b621cd70-8e5a-56ee-b272-20c6098bad66" :answer/id #uuid "b621cd70-8e5a-56ee-b272-20c6098bad66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬X∧¬Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9233ceaf-9f1c-5208-b9cd-674ecda2cce9" :answer/id #uuid "9233ceaf-9f1c-5208-b9cd-674ecda2cce9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5cc8fa9c-919a-5715-b893-d15125898b55" :question/id #uuid "5cc8fa9c-919a-5715-b893-d15125898b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-102732" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(¬P⇒Q).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬(¬(¬P)∨Q).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬P)∨Q)≡¬(P∨Q).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬P∧¬Q.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2d46aa30-73c8-590c-a750-bc1fe65993d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2d46aa30-73c8-590c-a750-bc1fe65993d7" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d46aa30-73c8-590c-a750-bc1fe65993d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07697fb7-beba-509d-9af4-221c0a6b4e35" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9820fe9-88d1-532e-b488-c19e1e9c8373" "answer-e0566476-5f2e-585a-a6da-7238085c09d7" "answer-85a8fd88-3b39-5857-b51b-e87763378f9b" "answer-07697fb7-beba-509d-9af4-221c0a6b4e35" "answer-da347817-d713-5445-9cf9-fd508c9e189a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9820fe9-88d1-532e-b488-c19e1e9c8373" :answer/id #uuid "a9820fe9-88d1-532e-b488-c19e1e9c8373" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P∧¬Q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0566476-5f2e-585a-a6da-7238085c09d7" :answer/id #uuid "e0566476-5f2e-585a-a6da-7238085c09d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P∨¬Q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85a8fd88-3b39-5857-b51b-e87763378f9b" :answer/id #uuid "85a8fd88-3b39-5857-b51b-e87763378f9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬P∧Q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07697fb7-beba-509d-9af4-221c0a6b4e35" :answer/id #uuid "07697fb7-beba-509d-9af4-221c0a6b4e35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬P∧¬Q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da347817-d713-5445-9cf9-fd508c9e189a" :answer/id #uuid "da347817-d713-5445-9cf9-fd508c9e189a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬P∨¬Q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060984 :tutorial/content "The conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor B{:}$ \n\n$$\nA \\Rightarrow B \\equiv \\lnot A \\lor B\n$$\n\nWe can verify this by the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nIn other words, any conditional statement of the form\n\n$\\qquad$ *if antecedent, then consequent*\n\ncan be expressed equivalently as \n\n$\\qquad$ *not antecedent, or consequent.*\n\nFor example, consider the following conditional statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If you do not take your watch, then you will be late for the meeting.* \n\nLet's rewrite this statement using its logically equivalent form. \n\nFirst, we denote the antecedent $A$ and consequent $B$ as follows:  \n\n- $A{:}$ You do not take your watch.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will be late for the meeting.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ is:\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You take your watch.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *You take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nWe can write this using more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nAnother way to write this is to append the word \"either\" to the beginning of the sentence, like so:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Either you take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nUsing the word \"either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(\\lnot A\\lor B)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059925 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
